# Audio

## Overview

The phone has three audio parts:

* the **loudspeaker**, a swappable module with its own amplifier,
* the **earpiece**, the small speaker you hold to your ear during a call,
* the **microphone**.

The earpiece and the microphone go through the WCD9335 audio codec. All
three are driven through the phone's audio DSP. Sound is played on
`plughw:0,0` and recorded from `hw:0,1`; ALSA mixer controls decide
whether playback goes to the loudspeaker or the earpiece.

Switch between them while nothing is playing: changing the route in the
middle of a playback stops it with an input/output error. To move a
sound that is playing, stop it, switch, and carry on from where it was;
[Switch while playing](#switch-while-playing) shows how.

The loudspeaker module is what differs between the Fairphone 3 and the
3+, and since it can be swapped, a phone sold as one may carry the
other's:

| Loudspeaker module | Amplifier | Sound card name |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Fairphone 3 | Awinic AW8898 | `Fairphone 3` |
| Fairphone 3+ | TI TAS2557 | `Fairphone 3+` |

Only the TAS2557 has a `Speaker Switch` control. This cell reads the sound
card name to know whether to set it. `ExAudio` sets the mixer controls;
`set` applies a list of them and shows the result of each one:

```elixir
tas2557? = File.read!("/proc/asound/cards") =~ "Fairphone 3+"

speaker_switch = fn value -> if tas2557?, do: [{"Speaker Switch", value}], else: [] end

set = fn controls ->
  for {name, value} <- controls, do: {name, ExAudio.cset(name, value)}
end

IO.puts("Loudspeaker amplifier: #{if tas2557?, do: "TAS2557", else: "AW8898"}")
```

## Loudspeaker

### Route audio to the loudspeaker

The firmware enables this route at boot. Run it again to come back from
the earpiece.

```elixir
speaker =
  [
    {"Earpiece Switch", :off},
    {"SLIMBUS_0_RX Audio Mixer MultiMedia1", :off},
    {"QUIN_MI2S_RX Audio Mixer MultiMedia1", :on}
  ] ++ speaker_switch.(:on)

set.(speaker)
```

### Play a tone

Generate one second of a 440 Hz tone, save it as a WAV file and play it
with `aplay`.

```elixir
rate = 48_000
volume = 0.1

tone = for n <- 0..(rate - 1), do: volume * :math.sin(2 * :math.pi() * 440 * n / rate)

NervesAI.Audio.WAV.write("/tmp/tone.wav", tone, rate: rate)
System.cmd("aplay", ["-D", "plughw:0,0", "/tmp/tone.wav"], stderr_to_stdout: true)
```

Change `volume` (0.0 to 1.0) and run it again.

### A short melody

```elixir
notes = [262, 294, 330, 349, 392, 440, 494, 523]

melody =
  for freq <- notes, n <- 0..(div(rate, 4) - 1) do
    volume * :math.sin(2 * :math.pi() * freq * n / rate)
  end

NervesAI.Audio.WAV.write("/tmp/melody.wav", melody, rate: rate)
System.cmd("aplay", ["-D", "plughw:0,0", "/tmp/melody.wav"], stderr_to_stdout: true)
```

## Earpiece

### Route audio to the earpiece

This turns the loudspeaker off and sends the same playback device to the
earpiece instead.

```elixir
earpiece =
  speaker_switch.(:off) ++
    [
      {"QUIN_MI2S_RX Audio Mixer MultiMedia1", :off},
      {"SLIMBUS_0_RX Audio Mixer MultiMedia1", :on},
      {"SLIM RX0 MUX", "AIF_MIX1_PB"},
      {"RX INT0_1 MIX1 INP0", "RX0"},
      {"RX INT0 DEM MUX", "CLSH_DSM_OUT"},
      {"RX0 Digital Volume", 84},
      {"EAR PA Volume", 4},
      {"Earpiece Switch", :on}
    ]

set.(earpiece)
```

### Listen

The earpiece is quiet: hold the top of the phone to your ear, then run
this to play the melody again.

```elixir
System.cmd("aplay", ["-D", "plughw:0,0", "/tmp/melody.wav"], stderr_to_stdout: true)
```

Run the loudspeaker route above to switch back.

## Switch while playing

A call app moves the sound between the earpiece and the loudspeaker while
it plays. The route can only change while nothing plays, so this player
feeds the sound to `aplay` a tenth of a second at a time. Switching stops
`aplay`, waits until the sound card has closed the stream, applies the
new route and starts a new `aplay` with the rest of the sound. There is
a short gap at the switch.

```elixir
defmodule SwitchablePlayer do
  @chunk_ms 100
  @prefill 3

  # Plays mono samples (floats between -1.0 and 1.0) at `rate` and
  # returns the player.
  def start(samples, rate) do
    pcm = for sample <- samples, into: <<>>, do: <<round(sample * 32_767)::little-signed-16>>
    spawn(fn -> play(pcm, rate) end)
  end

  # Moves the sound to another route: `route` is called while nothing plays.
  def switch(player, route), do: send(player, {:switch, route})

  defp play(pcm, rate) do
    port = open(rate)
    pcm = feed(port, pcm, rate, @prefill)
    loop(port, pcm, rate, System.monotonic_time(:millisecond), 0)
  end

  defp loop(port, <<>>, _rate, _t0, _n), do: send(port, {self(), :close})

  defp loop(port, pcm, rate, t0, n) do
    receive do
      {:switch, route} ->
        stop(port)
        route.()
        play(pcm, rate)
    after
      max(t0 + n * @chunk_ms - System.monotonic_time(:millisecond), 0) ->
        loop(port, feed(port, pcm, rate, 1), rate, t0, n + 1)
    end
  end

  defp feed(port, pcm, rate, chunks) do
    size = min(byte_size(pcm), div(rate * 2 * @chunk_ms, 1000) * chunks)
    <<chunk::binary-size(size), rest::binary>> = pcm
    Port.command(port, chunk)
    rest
  end

  defp open(rate) do
    aplay = System.find_executable("aplay")
    args = ~w(-q -D plughw:0,0 -t raw -f S16_LE -c 1 -r #{rate} -)
    Port.open({:spawn_executable, aplay}, [:binary, :exit_status, args: args])
  end

  # SIGKILL: on SIGTERM, an aplay waiting for more sound keeps waiting.
  defp stop(port) do
    {:os_pid, pid} = Port.info(port, :os_pid)
    System.cmd("kill", ["-KILL", Integer.to_string(pid)])

    receive do
      {^port, {:exit_status, _}} -> :ok
    after
      2_000 -> :ok
    end

    wait_closed(30)
  end

  # The card shuts the old route down after the stream closes, and the
  # device is busy until then.
  defp wait_closed(0), do: :timeout

  defp wait_closed(tries) do
    case File.read("/proc/asound/card0/pcm0p/sub0/status") do
      {:ok, "closed" <> _} -> :ok
      _ -> Process.sleep(100) && wait_closed(tries - 1)
    end
  end
end
```

Play the melody four times on the loudspeaker:

```elixir
set.(speaker)
player = SwitchablePlayer.start(List.flatten(List.duplicate(melody, 4)), rate)
```

While it plays, move it to the earpiece:

```elixir
SwitchablePlayer.switch(player, fn -> set.(earpiece) end)
```

And back to the loudspeaker:

```elixir
SwitchablePlayer.switch(player, fn -> set.(speaker) end)
```

## Microphone

### Route the microphone

This connects the phone's main microphone to the recording device.

```elixir
microphone = [
  {"MultiMedia2 Mixer SLIMBUS_0_TX", :on},
  {"SLIM TX7 MUX", "DEC7"},
  {"ADC MUX7", "DMIC"},
  {"DMIC MUX7", "DMIC1"},
  {"AIF1_CAP Mixer SLIM TX7", :on}
]

set.(microphone)
```

### Which microphone?

The phone has two microphones, both digital microphones on the codec, and
they sit at opposite ends:

| Input | Where | What it is for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `DMIC1` | bottom, next to the loudspeaker | the main microphone: voice and recordings |
| `DMIC2` | top, next to the earpiece | the second microphone, used to pick up background noise |

It is the same on the Fairphone 3 and the 3+. The route above selects
`DMIC1`. The codec's other inputs (`DMIC0`, `DMIC3` to `DMIC5` and the
analog ones) have nothing connected and record silence or noise.

`DMIC MUX7` picks the microphone. This shows which one is selected:

```elixir
dmics = ~w(ZERO DMIC0 DMIC1 DMIC2 DMIC3 DMIC4 DMIC5)
{:ok, index} = ExAudio.cget("DMIC MUX7")
IO.puts("Recording from #{Enum.at(dmics, String.to_integer(index))}")
```

And this switches. Pick `"DMIC2"`, run the recording below, then compare it
with one made on `"DMIC1"`: holding the top or the bottom of the phone
close to your mouth makes the difference obvious.

```elixir
use_microphone = fn mic -> set.([{"DMIC MUX7", mic}]) end

use_microphone.("DMIC2")
```

### Record

Records 5 seconds. Talk, clap or whistle while it runs.

```elixir
arecord = fn seconds, path ->
  System.cmd("arecord", ~w(-D hw:0,1 -f S16_LE -r 48000 -c 1 -d #{seconds} #{path}),
    stderr_to_stdout: true
  )
end

# The first capture after boot returns silence; a short one wakes the path up.
arecord.(1, "/tmp/warmup.wav")
IO.puts("Recording for 5 seconds...")
arecord.(5, "/tmp/me.wav")
```

### How loud was it?

The recording is split into tenths of a second, and each bar shows the
loudest sample in that slice.

```elixir
{:ok, %{rate: rate, pcm_f32: pcm}} = NervesAI.Audio.WAV.read("/tmp/me.wav")

for(<<sample::float-32-little <- pcm>>, do: abs(sample))
|> Enum.chunk_every(div(rate, 10))
|> Enum.map(fn slice ->
  peak = Enum.max(slice)
  String.duplicate("█", round(peak * 50)) <> " #{Float.round(peak, 2)}"
end)
|> Enum.join("\n")
|> IO.puts()
```

A silent room stays near 0; speaking close to the phone reaches 0.3 or
more.

### Play it back

Plays the recording on the loudspeaker:

```elixir
set.(speaker)
System.cmd("aplay", ["-D", "plughw:0,0", "/tmp/me.wav"], stderr_to_stdout: true)
```

Or in the earpiece:

```elixir
set.(earpiece)
System.cmd("aplay", ["-D", "plughw:0,0", "/tmp/me.wav"], stderr_to_stdout: true)
```

## Play your own file

`aplay` plays WAV files. Copy one to the phone (for example with
`sftp nerves.local`) and play it:

```elixir
System.cmd("aplay", ["-D", "plughw:0,0", "/data/my_sound.wav"], stderr_to_stdout: true)
```
